{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZSyYLNi4cYYmCL8CFYA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote a new candidate for the repair slot, based on a different mechanism than the one that was rejected. It passes the self-check (`OK`). Novelty against the other models, convergence and speed are only tested at admission, so none of those are verified yet.\n\n**Why it was rejected:** the previous attempt said each person has their own preferred switch rate. That is the same mechanism as `personal_ideal_alternation`, and its predictions were almost identical (RMSE 0.00008, far under the 0.002 limit). Two other models in the set also rest on individual alternation preferences, so I left that idea alone instead of trying another version of it.\n\n**New hypothesis, `online_transition_surprise`:** people read the sequence left to right and try to predict each flip from the one before it. As they go, they learn \"what follows H\" and \"what follows T\" from the flips seen so far. A sequence looks random to the extent it stays surprising to this learner.\n- Unlike the switch-rate models, flip order matters. A sequence whose transitions become predictable early (a streak, a strict alternation, a repeating habit) loses randomness, even if its overall alternation rate is moderate.\n- It has two free parameters: `alpha`, how strongly the learner holds its prior before committing to what it has seen, and `beta`, how strongly the difference in surprise drives the choice.\n- The model computes this step-by-step surprise itself from the raw sequences.\n\n**Self-check result:** the short smoke fit (one chain, 100 draws) gave an ELPD-LOO of −1556.0. That is well behind the current best, `local_representativeness` (−1398.4), and on a par with `falk_konold_dp` (−1579.4), which has already lost. So it may well be pruned at the end of the experiment. Since it follows a different, order-dependent mechanism, I expect it to 